<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    THIS FILE IS OURS. Everything it merges is vendored; this wrapper is not, and it carries no
    upstream copyright header because there is nothing of Splitwirez's in it. Every file it names
    does carry one — see LICENSE-aero-theme-for-avalonia.txt beside this file, and
    tests/TanMenu.Core.Tests/Themes/AeroAttributionTests.cs, which asserts the header mechanically
    over every vendored file under Themes/Aero/.

    ── What it is for ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The vendored colour layer is three files: 168 <Color> keys per variant, and 110 keys built from
    those names - 107 brushes plus three shared GradientStops collections - every colour in which is
    a {DynamicResource} onto a variant key rather than a literal. The brush file is therefore
    VARIANT-INDEPENDENT and the SAME file is merged into BOTH ThemeDictionaries, which is upstream
    AeroTheme.axaml's own arrangement, not an adaptation of it.

    ── Why the variant keys are named here and not upstream ─────────────────────────────────────
    Upstream keys its light dictionary "Default". This app keys its own "Light" (see
    Themes/Shell/Tokens.axaml), and ShellTokenTests.EveryThemedTokenIsDefinedInBothVariants
    reads that literal name out of Tokens.axaml. Merging the vendored files inside a wrapper WE
    write is what lets the outer key names stay ours while the values stay theirs — zero test change
    and no parser error standing in for a parity failure. The two vendored variant files declare the
    identical 168 x:Key names, so the "Light"/"Dark" pair below has no hole in it on day one.

    ── Load order, which is load-bearing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    App.axaml lists this file AFTER <FluentTheme /> and BEFORE Themes/Shell/Tokens.axaml.
    Avalonia.Styling.Styles.TryGetResource counts DOWN from Count-1, so Application.Styles is
    searched last-first: Launcher, then Tokens, then this, then FluentTheme. Aero therefore wins
    over Fluent, and our own overrides still win over Aero. That ordering is the whole reason
    ContentControlThemeFontFamily stays on Segoe UI rather than reverting to a font with no CJK
    coverage — and AVMAP/Metrics.axaml has that key deleted as well, so the guarantee does not rest
    on ordering alone.

    ── How this file arrived, in two steps ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Round 7 Task 2 vendored the resource layer above and wired it to NOTHING - no control theme, no
    style, no converter was referenced from here - so that a mistake in it would surface as a build
    error or as a pixel difference against Task 1's captures rather than as a silently wrong-looking
    app. Task 3 is what spent it, below. ALL THREE converters under Converters/ are spent:
    AdjustCornerRadiusConverter is instantiated three times (ControlStyles/Button_*, ListBox and
    ListBoxItem), ProgressBarFillBrushConverter once, as an x:Static singleton, by the progress
    bar's indicator fill, and AdjustThicknessForTabsConverter once, likewise as an x:Static
    singleton, by ControlStyles/TabItem.axaml's outer border. Four were vendored in Round 7; Task 5
    deleted ProgressBarAnimationConverter.cs and ProgressBarFillColorConverter.cs with the
    indeterminate machinery that was their only caller, and ROUND 8 TASK 2 VENDORED THE THIRD
    surviving one with the two tab dictionaries, because TabItem.axaml does not parse without it.

    ── The control themes, added by Round 7 Tasks 3, 4, 5, 6 and 9 and Round 8 Task 2 ────────────
    Sixteen ControlStyles dictionaries are merged below, VARIANT-INDEPENDENTLY: a control theme holds
    geometry and {DynamicResource} brush references, never a colour, so the same dictionary serves
    both variants and the brushes it names re-resolve when ActualThemeVariant changes. That is
    upstream AVMAP/Controls.axaml's own arrangement.

    Fifteen of the sixteen are keyed {x:Type X} and therefore auto-apply to every X that does not
    set Theme= itself. HeaderedContentControl is the exception and is OPT-IN, keyed "GroupBox",
    which is upstream's own choice rather than an adaptation: HeaderedContentControl is the base
    class of TabItem and of several other headered controls, so a {x:Type} key there would repaint
    things nobody asked for. Its consumers name it with Theme="{StaticResource GroupBox}".
    THAT CHOICE STOPPED BEING HYPOTHETICAL IN ROUND 8, TASK 2, which added a real
    {x:Type TabItem} theme: the two coexist precisely because GroupBox is not keyed by type, so the
    Options dialog's five tabs take TabItem.axaml and the six group boxes inside them keep taking
    HeaderedContentControl.axaml, with no rule reaching the other's subject.

    Counted against the tree - MARKUP AND CODE-BEHIND BOTH, because a control built in code has no
    Theme= to grep for - that reach is:

      - Button, TWO sites. AppPage.axaml's tm-update banner is the only one of the 26 <Button>
        elements in .axaml outside this folder with no Theme=; the other is
        Dialogs/MessageBoxWindow.axaml.cs, which builds the message box's one-to-three buttons in
        code and sets no Theme either. The first count of this file named only the banner and was
        wrong - the same class of miss the design had already flagged for CheckBox - so the message
        box was then driven to a real dialog and measured rather than assumed. Its buttons went from
        Fluent's 84 x 33.3 DIP pill to Aero's 84 x 22 DIP double border, and the alert window from
        170.7 to 159.3 DIP tall. MessageBoxWindow.axaml's own remark carries that measurement and
        the reason the change is wanted rather than tolerated.
      - ToggleSwitch, five: four in BehaviorCard.axaml and one in ToolsCard.axaml, none with Theme=.
      - CheckBox, none in markup: the ones ToolsCard.axaml.cs constructs per configured tool.
      - ComboBox, THREE, all in Views/Settings/AppearanceCard.axaml: LanguageCombo, AppearanceCombo
        and FontCombo, all non-editable and all Width="220". Task 6.
      - ComboBoxItem, none in markup either. LanguageCombo and AppearanceCombo bind an ItemsSource
        of strings, so their rows are containers Avalonia generates; FontCombo's rows are
        ComboBoxItems AppearanceCard.axaml.cs builds one per installed font family. A generated
        container and a hand-built one pick up a {x:Type} ControlTheme the same way - neither can
        carry a Theme= - which is what makes the code-built rows and the markup-built combos one
        look rather than two. Task 6.
      - ScrollViewer, TWO (AppPage and ToolsCard's per-tool list) plus the one inside this layer's
        own ListBox template, and every ScrollBar those instantiate. It was three until Round 8,
        Task 2 deleted Views/SettingsPage.axaml: a property page does not scroll (PUBLISHED,
        win-property-win, "Don't scroll property pages"), so the Options dialog has no scroller of
        its own and the settings surface's scrolling ScrollViewer went with the page.
      - TabControl, ONE, and TabItem, FIVE - Dialogs/OptionsWindow.axaml, the whole of Round 8
        Task 2's reason for vendoring them. Neither carries a Theme=.
      - ListBox, ONE navigation rail in MainWindow.axaml. It was both until Round 8, Task 2 deleted
        NavFooterRail: 设置 is a command that opens a dialog now, not a page the shell navigates to,
        so its row is a Button like the three commands above it.
      - TextBox, TWO: AppPage.axaml's SearchBox and BehaviorCard.axaml's HotkeyBox. Both carry a
        tm- CLASS rather than a Theme=, and a class does not stop a {x:Type} ControlTheme reaching
        them - it only adds rules on top. Added by Task 4.
      - NumericUpDown, ONE: AppearanceCard.axaml's ColCountUpDown, the 每列个数 spinner. Task 4.
      - ButtonSpinner, ONE and it is not in any markup: NumericUpDown's own template builds it.
        Task 4.
      - ProgressBar, TWO, both on the update path and neither with a Theme=: UpdatesCard.axaml's
        UpdateProgress on the 设置 page and AppPage.axaml's banner bar, which Task 5 turned from a
        hand-drawn Border-in-Border into a real ProgressBar. Both had Height="4" and both are 15
        now, for the reason each one's own remark states. Task 5.
      - HeaderedContentControl, SIX, and every one of them names the theme explicitly because this
        one is opt-in: the six group boxes on the 设置 page, one per file in Views/Settings. Task 9.

    Everything else is untouched BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by luck: the other 25 <Button> elements
    and both <ListBoxItem>s carry an explicit Theme=. Twenty-two of those Theme= values resolve to
    Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml; the remaining three are MainWindow.axaml's own caption buttons on
    TmCaptionButton, which that file declares in its own Window.Resources because it is window
    chrome rather than launcher dialect. (This paragraph named Launcher.axaml as the source of all
    25 until Round 7, Task 10.) The app's
    own markup contains no <RepeatButton> and no <ToggleButton> at all - the eight RepeatButtons in
    the tree are inside this layer's ScrollBar template, which themes them itself, and two more are
    inside the ButtonSpinner template, which gives them a theme of its own.

    ── The one thing Task 4 broke on purpose ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ControlStyles/TextBox.axaml replaces Fluent's TextBox template wholesale, and Aero's names its
    background border BgElement where Fluent's named it PART_BorderElement. Five rules in
    Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml reached for the Fluent name and would have gone silently dead;
    all five were fixed in the same commit as the merge below - all five re-targeted onto
    Border#BgElement, none deleted, and the search field's one gained a :pointerover twin because
    Aero repaints the background on hover as well - and
    ShellConstantTests.NoTemplatePartSelector_NamesAPartOutsideTheAllowList is what keeps the next
    occurrence loud. That file's own header is the authority on the change.

    StyleTrailMix is a <Styles>, not a dictionary, so it is a StyleInclude rather than a merge. It
    supplies the 125 ms brush cross-fade every Aero template's Border.aeroState2 asks for, and the
    AeroFadeDuration key that ControlStyles/CheckBox.axaml resolves by {StaticResource} - which
    works across files because Avalonia's StaticResourceExtension walks the parsing dictionary's
    parent stack and that stack ends at Application.Current, whose TryGetResource searches
    Application.Styles (decompiled from Avalonia.Markup.Xaml 12.1.0,
    AvaloniaXamlIlRuntimeHelpers.RootServiceProvider.CreateParentStackProvider).

    Three of the merges below stand in a different relation to upstream from the rest, and the three
    are not the same relation. ControlStyles/ProgressBar.axaml is the DETERMINATE BRANCH of a real
    upstream file, about 60 of the original's 471 lines, and its own header enumerates the ten
    differences rather than summarising them. ControlStyles/ComboBoxItem.axaml is a COPY OF A FILE
    WITH ANOTHER NAME: upstream has no drop-down row, but a control theme is keyed by the control's
    own type, so that file is the vendored ControlStyles/ListBoxItem.axaml re-keyed to ComboBoxItem
    - it carries the upstream notice and a six-item change list like any other copy.
    ControlStyles/ComboBox.axaml is the only one AUTHORED: the library has no ComboBox on any of its
    nine branches, so there is no upstream file it could be a copy of and no path a Source: line
    could name; its header names item by item the vocabulary it does reuse. AeroAttributionTests
    encodes exactly that split - ProgressBar and ComboBoxItem keep the notice, ComboBox alone is
    listed as not-vendored. HeaderedContentControl (Round 7, Task 9) is an ordinary
    copy and needs no note beyond its own header - but it brings the only vendored CONTROL in the
    tree with it, Controls/HeaderGapClipper.cs, without which its template draws the group label on
    top of an unbroken outline instead of in a notch cut out of it. TabControl and TabItem, the last
    two merges to land (Round 8, Task 2), are ordinary copies too - TabControl's header records
    "Changes from upstream: none.", TabItem's records the one re-pointed xmlns - and TabItem brings
    Converters/AdjustThicknessForTabsConverter.cs with it for the same kind of reason: without that
    file its template does not parse.

    ── The keys here that are not Aero-prefixed ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    66 keys are merged below (comments stripped, Metrics plus the sixteen control-style
    dictionaries). All but 35 are Aero-prefixed, and all 35 are wanted:

      - the SEVENTEEN {x:Type X} ControlTheme keys - Button, RepeatButton, ToggleButton (the Button
        file keys three), ButtonSpinner, CheckBox, ComboBox, ComboBoxItem, ListBox, ListBoxItem,
        NumericUpDown, ProgressBar, ScrollBar, ScrollViewer, TabControl, TabItem, TextBox,
        ToggleSwitch. A {x:Type} key is how a ControlTheme auto-applies at all, so these are the
        reach listed above;
      - GroupBox (ControlStyles/HeaderedContentControl.axaml), the one opt-in theme, keyed by name
        upstream and kept that way here for the reason given above;
      - ELEVEN inside ControlStyles/ButtonSpinner.axaml: two spinner arrow geometries, the
        ButtonSpinnerRightThickness margin converter and eight Spinner*Filter_Left/Right corner and
        thickness converters. All eleven are consumed by that one file's own template and by
        nothing else in this app - Fluent's ButtonSpinner template, the only other consumer of the
        first three names, is the template we just replaced;
      - ToggleSwitchTopHeaderMargin (ControlStyles/ToggleSwitch.axaml), a FLUENT key name redefined
        to Fluent 12.1's own value - Thickness 0,0,0,6, identical in both Controls/ToggleSwitch.xaml:19
        and the decompiled resource dictionary - so it reaches beyond what we template but cannot
        change anything. The other three Fluent-named global overrides upstream ships alongside it
        (ToggleSwitchThemeMinWidth, ToggleSwitchPreContentMargin, ToggleSwitchPostContentMargin) were
        dropped on vendoring; that file's header records why;
      - FIVE Fluent key names ControlStyles/TextBox.axaml redefines - TextBoxTopHeaderMargin,
        TextBoxClearButtonData, PasswordBoxRevealButtonData, PasswordBoxHideButtonData and
        DefaultTextBoxContextFlyout. All five are deliberately kept and only the last has any
        consequence; that file's header states it in full.
  -->

  <Styles.Resources>
    <ResourceDictionary>

      <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">
          <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
            <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/Variant/Light.axaml" />
            <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/AVMAP/Brushes.axaml" />
          </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        </ResourceDictionary>

        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Dark">
          <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
            <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/Variant/Dark.axaml" />
            <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/AVMAP/Brushes.axaml" />
          </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        </ResourceDictionary>

      </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

      <!-- Metrics is variant-independent: sizes, one corner radius, the scroll-bar thicknesses and
           the scroll expand easing. Merged outside the ThemeDictionaries for that reason, and so
           are the control themes below it, for the same reason.

           Metrics comes FIRST because ScrollBar.axaml and ScrollViewer.axaml resolve four of its
           keys with {StaticResource} (AeroScrollBarThumbMinimumExtent, AeroScrollBarLineButtonExtent
           are DynamicResource, but AeroScrollExpandDuration and AeroScrollExpandEase are not - a
           Transition's Duration and Easing are plain CLR properties, so no DynamicResource can
           reach them). Button comes before ScrollBar because ScrollBar's line-button themes are
           BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type RepeatButton}}", which Button_RepeatButton_ToggleButton
           declares. The order is otherwise upstream AVMAP/Controls.axaml's. -->
      <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/AVMAP/Metrics.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/Button_RepeatButton_ToggleButton.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ButtonSpinner.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/CheckBox.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ComboBox.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ComboBoxItem.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/HeaderedContentControl.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ListBox.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ListBoxItem.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/NumericUpDown.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ProgressBar.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ScrollBar.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ScrollViewer.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/TabControl.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/TabItem.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/TextBox.axaml" />
        <ResourceInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ToggleSwitch.axaml" />
      </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>

    </ResourceDictionary>
  </Styles.Resources>

  <StyleInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/AVMAP/StyleTrailMix.axaml" />

</Styles>
